// expect: 30
// mn-feedback-iir — W24 / LF.B gate: the `<~` prior-iteration BINDING.
//
// THE GENUINE DSP NOVELTY (PLAN §2, the `<~` arm): the feedback operator binds
// the PRIOR iteration's output as a live node the body reads. `(prev) => body`
// names that prior; the recurrence y[n] = f(x[n], y[n-1]) is EXPRESSED, not
// commented. This is the form Faust's untyped `~` reaches for — but here the
// prior is a typed graph node, read live from $__fb_prev_<h>, with NO closure
// allocation (a `<~` recurrence allocating per tick would defeat !Alloc).
//
// Integer IIR accumulator (float arithmetic is the seed's separate gap, STEP 1):
//   y[n] = x[n] + y[n-1],  x = 10 constant
//   y[0] = 10 + 0  = 10
//   y[1] = 10 + 10 = 20
//   y[2] = 10 + 20 = 30   <- the prior CARRIED across calls
// The state-global $s<h> persists the prior across iterative-context handler
// re-invocations; `(prev)` resolves to RFbPrior → LFeedbackPrior → the prior
// local. If the prior did NOT bind, every call returns 10 (the floor) and the
// result is 10, not 30 — so exit 30 proves the recurrence.
//
// THE SEED LAGS THIS FORM (the .mn LEADS, PLAN §5). The disposable seed's lower
// predates LF.B: its PFeedback arm lowers the `<~` LHS lambda as a CLOSURE
// (capturing `input`), never inlining the body with `prev` bound to the prior.
// So the seed-compiled result builds a closure record and never threads the
// prior — it computes the current input only. This gate therefore runs through
// MENTL2 (the wheel compiled by the seed — it carries the wheel's LF.B inline
// path: PFeedback → lower_feedback_body → ls_bind_fb_prior → LFeedbackPrior),
// NOT the seed directly. Named peer: Hβ.seed.feedback-prior-inline — the seed
// mirrors the LF.B lambda-inline when next required (STEP 5's coordinated seed
// edit); the .mn leads until then.
//
// LAW 7: the unbound `<~` form (a bare expression LHS, every prior structural
// filter) stays byte-identical — LF.B is an ADDED path keyed on a lambda LHS,
// not a perturbation of the existing emit.

effect Sample { sample_rate() -> Int }
effect IterContext { iter_context() -> Bool }

handler iterate_context {
  iter_context() => resume(True)
}

// The `<~` body reads the prior via its `(prev)` param — the recurrence.
fn accum(input) -> Int with Sample =
  ((prev) => input + prev) <~ delay(1)

fn run() -> Int with Sample = {
  let a = accum(10)
  let b = accum(10)
  let c = accum(10)
  c
}

fn main() -> Int = {
  { run() } ~> iterate_context
}
